1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,627
Every fall, millions of Americans rake up the best

2
00:00:03,627 --> 00:00:06,807
free soil builder their yard will ever produce.

3
00:00:06,807 --> 00:00:10,550
They stuff it into plastic bags, drag it to the curb,

4
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:14,293
and pay, one way or another, to have it hauled away.

5
00:00:14,752 --> 00:00:17,417
Then, come spring, they drive to the

6
00:00:17,417 --> 00:00:20,082
garden center and buy bags of compost.

7
00:00:20,082 --> 00:00:22,338
And a lot of that compost is

8
00:00:22,338 --> 00:00:24,593
made from, you guessed it, yard waste.

9
00:00:24,593 --> 00:00:26,672
Just like the leaves they threw out.

10
00:00:27,126 --> 00:00:29,467
And here's the part that should bother you a little.

11
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:33,644
According to the EPA, Americans generated about thirty-five million

12
00:00:33,644 --> 00:00:37,820
tons of yard trimmings in 2018, and roughly ten

13
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:41,533
million tons of that went straight into landfills.

14
00:00:41,996 --> 00:00:44,691
I'm gonna show you why fall leaves are basically

15
00:00:44,691 --> 00:00:47,386
free black gold, six ways we put them to

16
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:50,081
work here at ROOTED, and two mistakes that can

17
00:00:50,081 --> 00:00:52,177
turn all that gold into a problem.

18
00:00:52,177 --> 00:00:54,310
And stick around for the last method.

19
00:00:54,310 --> 00:00:57,442
Almost nobody uses it, and it builds a

20
00:00:57,442 --> 00:01:00,574
brand new garden bed for exactly zero dollars.

21
00:01:01,492 --> 00:01:04,037
First, why leaves are so valuable.

22
00:01:04,037 --> 00:01:06,718
A big tree's roots spread out way past

23
00:01:06,718 --> 00:01:09,399
the drip line, through far more soil than

24
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:12,081
your tomatoes or your lettuce will ever touch.

25
00:01:12,546 --> 00:01:15,531
All year, it pulls up calcium, magnesium and trace

26
00:01:15,531 --> 00:01:18,517
minerals, and a good share of that is still

27
00:01:18,517 --> 00:01:20,838
sitting in the leaves when they fall.

28
00:01:20,838 --> 00:01:24,317
Then every autumn, the tree drops it right at your feet.

29
00:01:24,776 --> 00:01:27,117
In a forest, nobody rakes.

30
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:30,927
The leaves fall, fungi and soil life break them down,

31
00:01:30,927 --> 00:01:34,736
and over a couple of years they turn into that

32
00:01:34,736 --> 00:01:37,783
dark, crumbly, sweet-smelling layer on the forest floor.

33
00:01:38,254 --> 00:01:40,065
Gardeners call it leaf mold.

34
00:01:40,065 --> 00:01:42,231
And honestly, it's about the best thing

35
00:01:42,231 --> 00:01:44,087
you can put into your soil.

36
00:01:44,556 --> 00:01:46,585
Now, let me be straight with you.

37
00:01:46,585 --> 00:01:49,795
Leaf mold is not high in nitrogen like

38
00:01:49,795 --> 00:01:52,604
manure, and it won't replace fertilizer overnight.

39
00:01:52,604 --> 00:01:53,881
That's not its job.

40
00:01:54,338 --> 00:01:57,114
Its superpower is structure and water.

41
00:01:57,114 --> 00:02:00,130
Good leaf mold soaks up water like a sponge.

42
00:02:00,130 --> 00:02:03,337
It's often said to hold several times its own weight.

43
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:05,722
Mix it into sandy soil, and your

44
00:02:05,722 --> 00:02:07,766
beds stop drying out by noon.

45
00:02:07,766 --> 00:02:10,572
Mix it into heavy clay, and it opens

46
00:02:10,572 --> 00:02:13,378
the clay up so roots can finally breathe.

47
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:17,852
It also feeds earthworms and the fungi your plants depend on.

48
00:02:17,852 --> 00:02:21,242
And healthy soil life is what turns any fertilizer you

49
00:02:21,242 --> 00:02:24,632
do use into something your plants can actually take up.

50
00:02:25,086 --> 00:02:27,169
So let's not be part of that number.

51
00:02:27,169 --> 00:02:29,887
Here are six ways to put those leaves to work.

52
00:02:30,806 --> 00:02:32,006
Method number one.

53
00:02:32,006 --> 00:02:33,745
Mow them right into your lawn.

54
00:02:33,745 --> 00:02:36,001
It's the easiest one on this list.

55
00:02:36,001 --> 00:02:37,332
You're mowing anyway.

56
00:02:37,804 --> 00:02:40,575
Instead of raking, run your mower over the

57
00:02:40,575 --> 00:02:43,346
leaves two or three times, until the pieces

58
00:02:43,346 --> 00:02:45,770
are about the size of a dime.

59
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:48,368
They settle down between the grass blades

60
00:02:48,368 --> 00:02:50,594
and disappear within a few weeks.

61
00:02:51,066 --> 00:02:54,039
Turf researchers at Michigan State University have

62
00:02:54,039 --> 00:02:56,587
studied this since the early nineties.

63
00:02:56,587 --> 00:03:00,296
Mowing leaves into the lawn didn't hurt the grass,

64
00:03:00,296 --> 00:03:04,005
and in their maple-leaf plots, dandelions actually went down.

65
00:03:04,005 --> 00:03:05,962
They're still not totally sure why.

66
00:03:06,416 --> 00:03:07,738
There's just one rule.

67
00:03:07,738 --> 00:03:09,776
If you can't see the grass through the

68
00:03:09,776 --> 00:03:11,814
leaves after you mow, there are too many.

69
00:03:11,814 --> 00:03:13,702
Rake up the extra and use it

70
00:03:13,702 --> 00:03:15,320
for one of the next methods.

71
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:17,440
Method number two.

72
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:19,016
The leaf mold cage.

73
00:03:19,016 --> 00:03:21,272
This is how you make real black gold.

74
00:03:21,726 --> 00:03:24,874
Grab a piece of chicken wire or hardware cloth,

75
00:03:24,874 --> 00:03:28,022
about ten to twelve feet long, and bend it

76
00:03:28,022 --> 00:03:30,820
into a circle three or four feet across.

77
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,287
Tie the ends together.

78
00:03:32,287 --> 00:03:33,334
That's your bin.

79
00:03:33,334 --> 00:03:36,085
Total cost, a few dollars, or nothing at

80
00:03:36,085 --> 00:03:38,836
all if you've got old fencing lying around.

81
00:03:39,308 --> 00:03:40,685
Fill it with leaves.

82
00:03:40,685 --> 00:03:43,022
And here's the step most people skip.

83
00:03:43,022 --> 00:03:44,421
Water them as you go.

84
00:03:44,421 --> 00:03:47,424
You want them damp, like a wrung-out sponge.

85
00:03:47,424 --> 00:03:49,924
Dry leaves will just sit there for years.

86
00:03:50,386 --> 00:03:53,502
If you can, run them over with the mower first.

87
00:03:53,502 --> 00:03:57,184
Shredded leaves break down much faster than whole ones.

88
00:03:57,648 --> 00:03:58,590
Quick tip.

89
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:01,076
Not all leaves rot at the same speed.

90
00:04:01,076 --> 00:04:04,031
Thin, soft leaves like maple, ash,

91
00:04:04,031 --> 00:04:06,987
cherry and birch break down fastest.

92
00:04:06,987 --> 00:04:09,595
Oak, beech and magnolia are tough and

93
00:04:09,595 --> 00:04:12,204
leathery, so they take a lot longer.

94
00:04:12,204 --> 00:04:16,660
Mix them, shred the tough ones, and your pile finishes faster.

95
00:04:17,126 --> 00:04:18,258
Then walk away.

96
00:04:18,258 --> 00:04:20,215
In about a year, the middle of

97
00:04:20,215 --> 00:04:22,171
the pile will be dark and crumbly.

98
00:04:22,171 --> 00:04:25,092
Give it two years, and you'll have leaf mold so

99
00:04:25,092 --> 00:04:28,013
fine you can use it in your seed starting mix.

100
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:29,967
So how do you know it's ready?

101
00:04:29,967 --> 00:04:31,203
Grab a handful.

102
00:04:31,203 --> 00:04:34,392
Finished leaf mold is dark brown, crumbly, and

103
00:04:34,392 --> 00:04:37,182
smells like a walk in the woods.

104
00:04:37,182 --> 00:04:38,812
If you can still make out

105
00:04:38,812 --> 00:04:40,442
whole leaves, give it more time.

106
00:04:40,442 --> 00:04:44,546
Half-done stuff is still great as mulch, so nothing's wasted.

107
00:04:44,994 --> 00:04:48,310
To use it, mix an inch or two into your

108
00:04:48,310 --> 00:04:51,626
beds before planting, spread it as mulch around your plants,

109
00:04:51,626 --> 00:04:54,278
or sift the finest stuff for seed starting.

110
00:04:54,278 --> 00:04:56,697
Your garden won't care how pretty it is.

111
00:04:57,602 --> 00:05:00,134
Method number three is the bag method.

112
00:05:00,134 --> 00:05:02,430
It's perfect if you're short on space.

113
00:05:02,896 --> 00:05:06,895
Fill a heavy black garbage bag with shredded, damp leaves.

114
00:05:06,895 --> 00:05:09,836
Poke a dozen holes in it with a screwdriver for

115
00:05:09,836 --> 00:05:12,778
air, tie it shut, and tuck it behind the shed.

116
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:16,957
Every few months, give it a shake and check the moisture.

117
00:05:16,957 --> 00:05:19,130
Shredded and kept damp, a lot of

118
00:05:19,130 --> 00:05:20,992
bags are ready by next fall.

119
00:05:20,992 --> 00:05:22,473
Some take a second year.

120
00:05:22,473 --> 00:05:25,252
Either way, you'll open it to a sack of leaf

121
00:05:25,252 --> 00:05:28,031
mold that would cost you real money at the store.

122
00:05:28,934 --> 00:05:30,216
Method number four.

123
00:05:30,216 --> 00:05:31,438
Mulch your beds.

124
00:05:31,438 --> 00:05:35,600
Spread two to three inches of shredded leaves over your

125
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:39,346
vegetable beds, and around your perennials, shrubs and garlic.

126
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:43,293
That blanket insulates the soil over winter, keeps the weeds down

127
00:05:43,293 --> 00:05:46,766
in spring, and by summer, the worms will have pulled a

128
00:05:46,766 --> 00:05:49,607
lot of it right into the ground for you.

129
00:05:50,058 --> 00:05:53,690
Just keep the leaves a few inches back from tree trunks

130
00:05:53,690 --> 00:05:56,992
and plant stems, so moisture doesn't sit against the bark.

131
00:05:56,992 --> 00:05:59,478
Oh, and hang on for number six.

132
00:05:59,478 --> 00:06:00,701
It's my favorite.

133
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:02,905
Method number five.

134
00:06:02,905 --> 00:06:04,916
Save some for your compost pile.

135
00:06:04,916 --> 00:06:08,381
Most compost piles have the opposite problem in summer.

136
00:06:08,381 --> 00:06:12,600
Too many kitchen scraps and grass clippings, not enough brown

137
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:16,397
material, and they turn into a smelly, soggy mess.

138
00:06:16,864 --> 00:06:18,721
So fill a few bags with

139
00:06:18,721 --> 00:06:20,578
dry leaves now and stash them.

140
00:06:20,578 --> 00:06:23,779
Next summer, every time you add a bucket of

141
00:06:23,779 --> 00:06:26,624
greens, add two or three buckets of leaves.

142
00:06:26,624 --> 00:06:29,953
That's roughly the balance a healthy compost pile wants.

143
00:06:30,864 --> 00:06:33,708
And method number six, the one I promised.

144
00:06:33,708 --> 00:06:36,554
Build a brand new garden bed right now,

145
00:06:36,554 --> 00:06:39,401
on top of your lawn, for zero dollars.

146
00:06:39,854 --> 00:06:41,000
Pick your spot.

147
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,926
Lay down plain brown cardboard, with the tape and labels pulled

148
00:06:44,926 --> 00:06:48,853
off, and overlap the edges so no grass can sneak through.

149
00:06:48,853 --> 00:06:50,592
Then soak it with the hose.

150
00:06:51,052 --> 00:06:54,915
Pile shredded leaves on top, eight to twelve inches deep.

151
00:06:54,915 --> 00:06:57,238
Mix in anything green you've got.

152
00:06:57,238 --> 00:07:00,153
The last grass clippings, coffee grounds from

153
00:07:00,153 --> 00:07:03,067
a local coffee shop, some kitchen scraps.

154
00:07:03,067 --> 00:07:05,384
The greens add nitrogen, and that

155
00:07:05,384 --> 00:07:07,700
helps the leaves break down faster.

156
00:07:08,154 --> 00:07:10,481
Top it with a thin layer of compost

157
00:07:10,481 --> 00:07:12,807
or soil if you have it, water it,

158
00:07:12,807 --> 00:07:14,843
and leave it alone for the winter.

159
00:07:15,296 --> 00:07:18,552
Under that blanket, the grass dies off, the

160
00:07:18,552 --> 00:07:21,401
cardboard softens, and the worms move in.

161
00:07:21,401 --> 00:07:24,105
By spring, it'll have settled way down.

162
00:07:24,105 --> 00:07:27,471
Pull back the top layer, tuck your transplants into

163
00:07:27,471 --> 00:07:30,463
a scoop of compost or soil, and plant.

164
00:07:30,463 --> 00:07:33,087
Tough runners like Bermuda grass may poke through,

165
00:07:33,087 --> 00:07:35,382
so keep an eye on the edges.

166
00:07:35,854 --> 00:07:38,696
A lot of folks skip soaking the cardboard, and

167
00:07:38,696 --> 00:07:41,538
come spring it's still stiff as a pizza box.

168
00:07:41,538 --> 00:07:42,720
So soak it good.

169
00:07:43,638 --> 00:07:46,441
Now, the two mistakes I warned you about.

170
00:07:46,892 --> 00:07:48,187
Mistake number one.

171
00:07:48,187 --> 00:07:50,728
Piling up thick layers of whole leaves.

172
00:07:50,728 --> 00:07:54,906
Big leaves like maple can mat together into a wet,

173
00:07:54,906 --> 00:07:59,084
airless sheet that sheds water and smothers whatever is underneath.

174
00:07:59,084 --> 00:08:02,603
Shred them, or at least mix them up with other material.

175
00:08:03,058 --> 00:08:04,394
Mistake number two.

176
00:08:04,394 --> 00:08:07,750
Putting fresh black walnut leaves where they don't belong.

177
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:10,852
They contain juglone, a compound that can

178
00:08:10,852 --> 00:08:13,511
stunt tomatoes and other sensitive plants.

179
00:08:13,511 --> 00:08:14,734
Don't mulch with them.

180
00:08:14,734 --> 00:08:18,130
Compost them in a hot, moist pile, and give it

181
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:21,187
about six months before it goes near your veggies.

182
00:08:21,648 --> 00:08:23,989
And one thing you can stop worrying about.

183
00:08:23,989 --> 00:08:26,030
A lot of people think oak leaves

184
00:08:26,030 --> 00:08:27,780
will make their soil too acidic.

185
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:30,509
In practice, once they break down, the

186
00:08:30,509 --> 00:08:32,848
effect on soil pH is small.

187
00:08:32,848 --> 00:08:34,152
Oak leaves are great.

188
00:08:34,152 --> 00:08:35,035
Use them.

189
00:08:35,936 --> 00:08:37,231
So here's the plan.

190
00:08:37,231 --> 00:08:38,250
Mow some in.

191
00:08:38,250 --> 00:08:39,948
Cage some for leaf mold.

192
00:08:39,948 --> 00:08:41,171
Mulch your beds.

193
00:08:41,171 --> 00:08:42,829
Bag a few for next summer.

194
00:08:42,829 --> 00:08:44,568
And if you've got the energy,

195
00:08:44,568 --> 00:08:46,307
start a new bed for free.

196
00:08:46,774 --> 00:08:50,121
Your trees spent all summer gathering that stuff for you.

197
00:08:50,121 --> 00:08:51,568
The least we can do is

198
00:08:51,568 --> 00:08:53,015
not put it on the curb.

199
00:08:53,484 --> 00:08:55,660
Tell me in the comments what you do with

200
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:57,836
your leaves, and what kind of trees you've got.

201
00:08:57,836 --> 00:08:59,249
I really do read them.

202
00:08:59,249 --> 00:09:02,503
And if you want more forgotten, zero-cost ways

203
00:09:02,503 --> 00:09:05,350
to build living soil, subscribe to ROOTED.

204
00:09:05,350 --> 00:09:06,491
Happy gardening.
